  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1114 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms continue across far southeast Nebraska\nand southwest Iowa late this evening. These showers and\nthunderstorms are ahead of a cold front that has been slowly\nmoving east through the region. This front is expected to clear\nthe area around midnight, bringing cooler and quieter weather \nbehind it. Temperatures cool into the 40s across much of the \narea with any 50s confined to portions of southeast Nebraska and\nsouthwest Iowa Thursday morning. Light winds and clear skies \nmay result in patchy shallow fog, but this threat remains low. \nLow-level moisture remains very shallow behind the front as \ncold, drier air filters in from the north. Temperatures warm \ninto the 60s by Thursday afternoon with plentiful sunshine for a\npleasant Fall day.\n\nOur next weather system begins to approach the region heading\ninto Friday. The previously mentioned cold front will stall just\nsouth of our area across the Central Plains. At the same time, a\nshortwave trough will lift northeast from the Southern Rockies\ninto the Central Plains. Warm advection and isentropic ascent\nahead of this feature helps to initiate thunderstorms late\nThursday evening into early Friday morning. Any thunderstorms \ngradually spread north heading into Friday afternoon as the \nweather system continues to approach the area. Unfortunately, \nportions of northeast Nebraska may miss out on this rainfall. \nTemperatures on Friday remain similar to Thursday with the front\nremaining stalled to the south of the area and increasing cloud\ncover. \n\nThe aforementioned weather system begins to move east of the\narea heading into Saturday, Halloween. Rain chances taper off\nthrough the afternoon with the evening of Halloween appearing \ndry. This is welcomed news for anyone partaking in trick-or- \ntreating. Temperatures remain in the 60s during Saturday \nafternoon, cooling into the upper 50s by evening. Sunday sees \nthe return of quiet weather as we move into a lull between \nweather systems.\n\nWeather early next week appears active. Model guidance is in\nrelatively good agreement that a strong upper-level low pressure\nwill take shape over the Central Plains on Monday heading into\nTuesday. As this occurs, widespread rain chances are\nanticipated. At this time, our region remains along the center\nof the track of this system, thus keeping temperatures cooler\nwith more of a steady rainfall. Rainfall could still be heavy \nat times with modified tropical moisture helping to feed this \nsystem. That being said, a more northerly track could bring \nwarmer temperatures and thunderstorms into the forecast. There \nremains some uncertainty with the exact evolution of this \nsystem, so stay tuned as the finer details become clearer this \nweekend. In either event, cooler weather filters in behind this \nweather system by the middle of the week allowing 50s to return to\nthe forecast.",
